  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Delaware received $161M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government recorded 2,808 awards in the state during the period, with an average award size of $57.3K. Obligations fell 56.3% year over year. Historical totals show that Delaware’s federal obligations have varied across recent fiscal years: FY21 was $321M, FY22 was $81.3M, FY23 was $325M, FY24 was $337M, FY25 was $368M, and FY26 is $161M to date. The Department of Defense is the single largest buyer in FY2026 to date, obligating $72.6M, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs with $31.1M and the Department of Health and Human Services with $16.9M. Industry concentration in FY2026 to date is led by Other Computer Related Services at $17.8M and Petroleum Refineries at $13.1M. Top vendors in the state are led by STONEWIN INTERNATIONAL, LLC with $13.1M, AQUIA NAVA II LLC with $11.8M, and CORBALT, LLC with $10.8M. Current-year totals are partial, reflecting fiscal year to date reporting.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal spending in Delaware in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $72.6M, which is the largest single-agency buyer. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $31.1M and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $16.9M, showing the top three agencies account for a substantial share of the $161M total. Obligations fell 56.3% year over year, and the FY2026 total of $161M is lower than the FY25 total of $368M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors lead federal contracting in Delaware in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Other Computer Related Services is the largest industry with $17.8M, followed by Petroleum Refineries with $13.1M and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences with $12.8M. STONEWIN INTERNATIONAL, LLC leads vendors with $13.1M, AQUIA NAVA II LLC follows with $11.8M, and CORBALT, LLC follows with $10.8M."
      }
